Thumbs.db
.DS_Store
*.prenav
*.bak

# Restore bundles hold WireGuard private keys, Wi-Fi passphrases and the
# console's write password. They must never reach a public repository.
backups/
*.tar.gz
*.packages

# Live router state, in case a copy is ever pulled off the device into this tree
# to look at. DENY BY DEFAULT, allow the few files that genuinely belong here.
#
# This was a list of names, and a list of names goes stale the moment anything is
# added — which it did, inside a single session: repeater-known (Wi-Fi passphrases)
# had to be remembered by hand, while oui and nlbw-daily were simply missed, and
# so was every /etc/config file. The worst of those is etc/config/network, which
# is where WireGuard private keys live, with etc/config/wireless holding the
# Wi-Fi passphrases beside it.
#
# Written as "ignore the directory, then re-admit what is tracked" so that a file
# added later is private unless someone deliberately says otherwise. That is the
# safe direction for a public repository: the failure mode of the old form was a
# silent publish, the failure mode of this one is a file that needs an explicit
# exception before it can be committed.
etc/config/
etc/dashboard/*
!etc/dashboard/classmap.example
etc/nftables.d/
